[2/200] Running: Explain the architecture of a transformer model....

🧠 LLM Response:

Answer: The architecture of a transformer model is based on multi-head self-attention and feed-forward neural network. There are no recurrent or convolutional layers in the model. The model has an encoder-decoder architecture.

Encoder:
The encoder is composed of a stack of identical layers, with each layer having two sub-layers: a multi-head self-attention layer and a position-wise feed-forward network. Each of these sub-layers is followed by a residual connection and a normalization layer.

Decoder:
The decoder also consists of a stack of identical layers. Each layer in the decoder has three sub-layers: a multi-head self-attention layer, an encoder-decoder attention layer, and a position-wise feed-forward network. Again, residual connections and normalization layers are used after each sub-layer.

Multi-Head Self-Attention:
This module calculates the attention score between each word in the input sequence and the other words in the sequence. This score is used to compute a weighted sum of the input words, which is then passed through a linear layer. The transformer model uses multiple such attention heads, each of which has a different weight matrix, allowing it to focus on different aspects of the input.

Positional Encoding:
Since the transformer model doesn't have recurrence, it can't keep track of the position of words in a sentence. To overcome this, positional encoding is added to the input embeddings before they are fed into the model. These encodings are vectors that indicate the position of a word in the sequence.

In summary, the architecture of a transformer model is based on multi-head self-attention, feed-forward neural networks, residual connections, and normalization layers, along with positional encoding to handle the position of words in a sequence. The model has an encoder-decoder architecture, with the encoder and decoder composed of stacked identical layers.
