CNN is designed for locally-connected networks of neurons. The novelty of CNN lies in its sharing of parameters in different local regions. After all, what we do is to extract features, and, for a $W \times H \times D$ image, we generally extract features that are shift-invariant. This requires that similar feature detectors should be available at different positions within the image so we can recognize that feature wherever it is in the image.

That is similar from what we have in the Convolution, the same filter is used on different regions of the input image. Moreover, the pooling operator is employed to reduce the spatial resolution of the CNN, which improves the recognition performance while reducing the computational cost and the number of parameters to be learned.

RNNs, on the other hand, they are not required to share parameters but are designed to work in sequences. They are well exploited in settings where the order of the input data is of relevance, using the previous data for computing the next data. This recalls the usage of memory states, which can be seen as stored data from the past that are utilized for current computations. For this reason, they can understand long-term dependencies and can model sequences with time lags.

