```

### High Memory Efficiency

DeepSpeed handles OOM and efficient utilization of memory. With the ZDP feature of DeepSpeed, memory is allocated for the entire batch whereas parameter gradients are retained only on each GPU (occupy 4--5 bytes/parameter). With the activation memory partition, significantly less memory is required on each GPU leading to significantly better utilization for memory hungry models. This ensures an efficient process to train large models even in highly limited constrained GPU memory scenarios.

### Improved Throughput

With the implementation of the ZDP feature, there is no impact in inference
[20/200] Running: Compare data parallelism and pipeline parallelism....

🧠 LLM Response:


Data parallelism is a parallelism strategy that refers to the distribution of data across different processors, each of which performs the same computation on different parts of the data at the same time. This strategy is often used in machine learning applications, where a large dataset is divided into smaller subsets, and each subset is processed in parallel by a different processor. The results are then combined to produce the final output.

Pipeline parallelism, on the other hand, is a parallelism strategy that refers to the distribution of different stages of a computation across different processors, where each stage performs a different computation on the same data. This strategy is often used in data processing pipelines, where data flows through a series of stages, with each stage performing a different operation on the data before passing it on to the next stage.

In summary, data parallelism distributes data across multiple processors that perform the same computation, while pipeline parallelism distributes different stages of a computation across multiple processors that operate on the same data.
