Embeddings are a type of word representation that allows words with similar meaning to have a similar representation. They are used in NLP models to convert text data into a numerical format that the model can understand.

Embeddings are learned during the training process of a NLP model, by optimizing a loss function. The embedding matrix is a matrix where each row corresponds to a word and each column corresponds to a dimension of the embedding space. During training, the model adjusts the values in the embedding matrix to minimize the loss function.

Embeddings can capture semantic and syntactic similarities between words. For example, words that are used in similar contexts will have similar embeddings, even if they are not spelled similarly or do not have a direct lexical relationship. This property makes embeddings useful for a variety of NLP tasks, such as text classification, sentiment analysis, and machine translation.

In summary, embeddings are a way to represent words as vectors, where similar words have similar vectors. They are an essential component of many NLP models because they allow the models to understand text data in a numerical format and capture the relationships between words.
[30/200] Running: Describe the function of the tokenizer's vocabulary....

🧠 LLM Response:


The tokenizer's vocabulary serves as the interface between the tokenizer and the model in terms of string manipulation. When tokens are added to or removed from the tokenizer, the vocabulary updates the corresponding index-to-token and token-to-index mappings. At runtime, the tokenizer maps input strings (or WordPieces fragments) to their integer tokens by referring to the vocabulary. Similarly, when token IDs are decoded back into strings, the vocabulary is used again to find the token corresponding to a given ID. This allows the tokenizer to abstract away the underlying integer representations of tokens and focus on string manipulation.
