[40/200] Running: What is distillation in model compression?...

🧠 LLM Response:


- Distillation is a knowledge transfer technique from model compression that transfers knowledge from a more complex, well-trained teacher model to a smaller student model
- The idea is that a well-trained teacher model has learned complex patterns during training from the data, and it is fruitful to transfer this knowledge when training a student model that has less capacity
- Originally, distillation used human-provided soft targets for labels; more recently (Bucilua et al, 2006; Hinton et al, 2015), soft-targets produced by teacher models were used instead to train student models
- Distillation is significantly more general and powerful than previous knowledge transfer techniques like initializing weights or adding a regularization term in the loss function

References:

- Bucilua, R. F., Caruana, R. A., & Niculescu-Mizil, A. (2006). Model compression. In ICML (pp. 1077-1084).
- Hinton, G., Vinyals, O., & Dean, J. (2015, June). Distilling the knowledge in a neural network. In International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML) (pp. 1-10).
