Machine learning modes can be run in many ways, ranging from embedded sensor nodes to high-performance servers. Streaming inference (also known as real-time inference, or embedded inference) is the execution mode that makes predictions based on continuous, real-time data streams.

Real-time processing can be used, for example, in industrial automation or safety applications, such as robot vision. The machine learning process must keep up with the sensor, so that decisions can be made on the fly by making fast predictions about incoming data.

DeepStream inference engine is designed to perform element-by-element processing of machine learning models, such as YOLO and SSD networks, in a way that is optimized for real-time NVIDIA GPU performance.

The NVIDIA TensorRT engine is optimized for inference on all NVIDIA GPUs, from the embedded Jetson platforms to the NVIDIA high-performance servers.

Specific features of the TensorRT inference engine enable batch sorting and pipeline management. These features allow TensorRT to combine multiple inference requests into a single batch for submission to the GPU. Each request in a batch is delivered by a pipeline, which performs preprocessing and postprocessing according to the user’s needs. When performing a streaming or near-streaming inference, DeepStream interacts with the TensorRT engine via the deepstream-plugin API to ensure the synchronous processing of incoming buffers.

The diagram shows how the DeepStream pipeline selects the best TensorRT network model based on the size of the incoming frame to maximize GPU utilization, and how DeepStream can perform inference from multiple models in parallel, with synchronous processing and high performance.

