[45/200] Running: How does TTFT relate to user-perceived latency?...

🧠 LLM Response:


The First-Frame deadline in TTFT defaults to 100 milliseconds for desktop browsers, because unsuitable request coalescing can cause user-perceived latency, even if the request is speculatively executed, and the speculative result is delivered to the user in a short time. Recent user-perceived latency research has revealed to web developers that even milliseconds of delay are noticeable to users. This research also led web developers to understand that the optimal latency threshold is around 100 ms [1,2]. Which is the reason for the default of the First-Frame Loading deadline in TTFT.

However, TTFT doesn't directly measure user-perceived latency [3], but instead measures the request coalescing time and the response time of an early document loaded by the browser. It's a conservative metric, which is a good thing given the browser is carrying a large amount of work speculatively.

TTFT changes how the browser works speculatively. Ideally, this would mean improved user-perceived latency. In fact, research shows that users can notice the speed of web pages [4], and even a modest improvement in speed leads to a significant improvement in link bounce rates [5]. This is the hope of those who adopt TTFT.

