Token-by-token generation is a way of creating machine-generated text by predicting the next word or token given a sequence of previous words or tokens. The prediction for the next token is made sequentially, from left to right, until a sentence-ending symbol is generated. While this approach has been successful in various NLP tasks such as machine translation and summarization, it has some challenges as well. Some of these challenges include 1) propagation of errors, in which a mistake in the early prediction can lead to a cascade of errors in later predictions, 2) difficulty in capturing long-range dependencies, as the prediction of each token only considers the previous tokens within a short window, 3) lack of control over the structure of the generated text, and 4) inconsistency in the generated text, where repeating the same input sequence can result in different outputs.
