[52/200] Running: How do transformers process multilingual text?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 Transformers are deep learning models that pre-train on large amounts of data by trying to predict missing text chunks or proper responses. In this process, transformers encode the input text (or ‘write’ the input text in its vector space), transfer the encoded sequence to a decoder (or ‘read’), and predict the next sequence of the sentence.

When the input text consists of multiple sentences, transformers first divide every word into tokens (e.g. using Byte Pair Encoding), add two special tokens (‘cls’ for the classification token and ‘sep’ for separate), and then encode each word vector based on a specific number of vectors. The decoder then selects the ‘cls’ vector to make predictions.

But how do transformers deal with multilingual text without conceptualizing or losing the distinctions between different languages? By introducing a language selector, the transformer can distinguish text from different training sets and learn internal representations for different languages before feeding them into the main model.

The language selector is added to a multilingual pre-trained transformer (e.g. mBERT) by adding an extra fully-connected feed-forward layer that chooses how to process tokens based on their level of language similarity. The feed-forward layer is added after the max pooling layer of transformers and has two options: a diluted transformation matrix and a concatenation.

The diluted transformation matrix is a transformation layer with the same size as the transformer’s output dimension. It has learnable parameters that select sentences that show up in training and select non-essential sentences from the same language in testing. Using the diluted transformation matrix weighs the original language transformation parameters by lambda and insulates them from being altered when training on different languages.

The concatenation option concatenates each token with a one-hot representation of its corresponding language and uses a larger transformation matrix that is adjusted only for parameters that are not related to a specific language. The concatenation option scales the entire transformation matrix by the number of languages plus the original weight vector length of the base transformer.

To evaluate the performance of the language selector, the team pre-trained the transformer on over 100 languages. The language selector attained a higher translation performance compared to a single model when translating between or into low-resource languages or less-related languages. By learning representations for each language within a language selector, the transformer can learn language distinctions during pre-training and carefully compare words in the same language but with potentially different weights.

To build an example of using transformers to process multilingual text, here is an example taken from the official Hugging Face transformers book. Hugging Face transformers (named Transformers) is a software library that makes it easier to have and design models for natural language processing tasks. The following example shows two functions for translating text using the multi-lingual mBERT pre-trained model.

```
import torch
from transformers import AutoTokenizer, AutoModelForSeq2SeqLM

tokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained("Helsinki-NLP/opus-mt-en-xx")
model = AutoModelForSeq2SeqLM.from_pretrained("Helsinki-NLP/opus-mt-en-xx")

def tokenize_transliterate(input_text):
    return tokenizer.encode_plus(       input_text,
        padding="max_length", 
        truncation_strategy="longest_first")
    
def translate_text(model, input_text):
    tokenized_text = tokenize_transliterate(input_text)   
    outputs = model.generate(**tokenized_text, 
         max_length=tokenized_text["input_ids"].size(1) * 2, 
         early_stopping=True)
        
    return tokenizer.decode(outputs[0])

text = "Transformers process multilingual text."
translation = translate_text(model, text) # output: "Los transformers procesan textos multilingües."
```

Transformers process multilingual text by using language selectors. These language selectors override the model’s original behavior and provide internal representations for a specific language. Using these representations, it can translate or classify any text regardless of the language, the first example they are trained on, or the language relationship.

