[70/200] Running: What are tensor parallel and tensor slicing?...

🧠 LLM Response:


When working with large tensor models on a single device, you quickly run into memory issues and limits on how large of a batch size you can use for training. Clearly, for these large models to be trained, parallelism of some sort is required.

Tensor parallelism speeds up the training by distributing the weights of the tensor model across the different GPUs available and performing computations in parallel on these. However, distributing the weights across different GPUs completely changes how the nodes of the model are connected to each other. This is also the reason why we need to use a network of Torch script/JIT to create the model in the first place. This parallel process is called tensor parallelism as it parallels the tensor operations and is implemented by giving weights specific places in the tensor model and communicating changes to these weights via the cross-GPU connection. After the weights have been split across multiple GPUs, the operations of the model will be distributed, thereby effectively increasing the available computational power. A common implementation of tensor parallel techniques is the Megatron-LM model that is used to train large models.

Tensor slicing is another parallelisation strategy similar to. The idea behind this parallelisation is to slice the data in smaller batches that can be trained. This technique is adopted by many training libraries that support parallelism, such as Apex from NVidia and the HorovodRunner and Horovod from Uber . By slicing the data, the parallel processors can process this data in one or more passing, whereby each processor will receive a part of the data and then send the results to the other processors. Each processor will have access to only a part of the data but calculations are performed in parallel over the full data. The coordination between the processors is usually done using a master that will coordinate the start and end of the job, helping keeping track with which processor which data is processed, and thereby organizes how the data will be passed between the processors. It also coordinates MPI (Message Passing Interface) instructions amongst the processors, which is the main way the processors can communicate with eachother. Tensor slicing is usually paired with model parallelism (distributing weights across multiple GPUs), which means the weights are on each processor while doing this data slicing.

Conclusion:

Tensor parallel is a method of sharing the weights of a neural network across multiple GPUs to increase computational power available during training. The computations are split according to the number of processors available and performed in parallel to effectively increase available computational power. Tensor Slicing is a method to divide the data into smaller chunks which are trained in parallel by the processors. There are two main types of parallel approaches:

1. Data parallelism, which duplicates the model locally on each GPU and uses a different subset of the dataset for training.
2. Model parallelism, which distributes the model weight matrices over multiple GPUs to be able to fit the entire model into memory.

When combined, this allow for much larger models to be handled by the available infrastructure. The most common implementation of these techniques is done by the Megatron-LM library, which can train models with hundreds of billions of parameters.
