This benchmark suite covers several popular NLP tasks across a range of languages. We use the input locally and output its predictions, notion, and model scores online.

## Tasks
The supported tasks are as follows.
### Textual Entailment

The textual entailment task is to predict whether a hypothesis can be inferred from a given premise. An example is given in Table 1. The first two rows are the input premise and hypothesis, and the third row shows that the hypothesis can be inferred from the premise (entailment) and assigns an entropy score of 0.375. A popular dataset to evaluate textual entailment performance is the Stanford Natural Language Inference (SNLI) dataset (<https://nlp.stanford.edu/projects/snli/>) (Bowman et al., 2015). The SNLI dataset contains 550K human-generated English pairs of premises and hypotheses, with 570K labels (entailment, neutral, or contradiction).

### Named Entity Recognition (NER)

Named Entity Recognition (NER) is a sequence labeling task to identify and categorize named entities in a text. The entities fall into several categories, such as person names, organization names, and location names. An example is given in Table 2. The first row is the input text, and the second row shows the golden labels. The third row gives the output of mBERT-base, and we can see that the mBERT-base model correctly recognizes named entities ("John", "Chicago", and "City council"). The widely used NER dataset is CoNLL03 (<https://www.clips.uantwerpen.be/conll2003/ner/>) [Tjong Kim Sang and De Meulder, 2003]. CoNLL03 is an English NER dataset that contains 1,393 training and 345 test news articles, with 4 human-annotated named entity categories (person, organization, location and miscellaneous).

### Question Answering (QA)

The question answering (QA) task is to predict an answer or extract the span from a text given a question about the text. The input includes a text and a question, as shown in Table 3. The first two rows show the input text and question. The third row shows four possible answers (A, B, C, and D), and the model should predict the correct one (B). If the model can extract a span from the input text (highlighted in Table 4), there is no need to give possible answers. An example is shown in Table 4. The input text is different from that in Table 3, but the question is the same. The model should predict the same answer, which is the span (Matt Damon), as shown in Table 4. The commonly used QA dataset is the SQuAD dataset (<https://rajpurkar.github.io/SQuAD-explorer/>) [Rajpurkar et al., 2016]. SQuAD is a question answering dataset involving predicting spans in wikipedia articles that answer the questions.


