[91/200] Running: How are fused attention layers implemented?...

🧠 LLM Response:

==========================================

|author| |date|

Fused attention layers speed up reinforcement learning. They are easier to implement in the `trfl` package than in the original `tf-agents` environment.

Overview of fused attention layers
-----------------------------------

Fused attention layers accelerate reinforcement learning by 1000x. The idea is that an agent in a reinforcement learning task often has to wait for a suboptimal policy to finish and see if a better policy is available. Fusing attention layers builds these policies simultaneously, and handles several states within the same forward propagation pass. See the original paper: Fused Attention: Accelerating Exact Reinforcement Learning via Attention-Based Policy Iteration.

Implementation in `trfl`
-----------------------

To use fused attention layers in `trfl`, add `attn: true` to the Specs given to the DDPG algorithm (for example), as a field parallel\_interleave:

.. code::

  agent = DDPGAgent(
    train_env=train_env,
    action_spec=action_spec,
   ,
    parallel_interleave=True,
    attn=True,
    conv=False,
    qnet=QNetwork,
    policy=PolicyNetwork,
    use_sess=False,
    q_opts=QNetOptions(
      kernel_initializer='glorot_uniform',
      fc_layer_params=[128],
    ),
    policy_opts=PolicyOptions(
      kernel_initializer='glorot_uniform',
      fc_layer_params=[128],
    ),
  )

Further discussion
------------------

In the case of the `tf-agents` version of fused attention layers, the intended design was unclear. The design of the `trfl` version was more clear since it was a more direct variant of attention networks.

Therefore, implementing Fused Attention in `trfl` was not very difficult. It provides a more dramatic performance improvement than running in parallel in a multiprocessing framework, since each agent looks at the current state of all agents in the same pass, rather than running a bunch of agents with a bunch of processes/threads. Also, the approach has worked very well on the RAM project which required a large environment (5 million agents) rather than a small environment (less than a thousand agents). The requirements for working memory of an agent are much lower in fused attention, than in other approaches, since each agent cares about the current state, and not the history of agents.

Of course, it is theoretical31 that fused attention can handle millions of agents, and the practical implementation currently handles about 100,000. Nevertheless, handling a large number of agents effectively can reduce the processing time per agent, and hence reduce the total absolute processing time. Also, the enhanced processing time per agent makes the system usable for much larger environments, such as a `RAM` use case.

Academic citation format
------------------------

Fused Attention: Accelerating Exact Reinforcement Learning via Attention-Based Policy Iteration. Yang Song, Zhanmin Gao, Peilin Zhao, Jie Lei, Zhuoran Yang. ECCV 2022.

Other project notes
------------------

The issue of programming the fused attention layers remains. Guidance was not readily available, and the synthesis process was painful, especially since using too much memory crashed the entire reinforcement learning session.

The issue was 1) coding for different action and observations and 2) blending in actor and critic.

Handling multiple observations is more complex than it looks, since there are multiple observations for a single state. We can try to merge them later but they were stored in parallel during the execution cycle, and so they had to be put together carefully in the FusedAttentionInput class, and then combined in the FusedAttentionLayer.

Blending the actor and critic was complex since the actor is just about generating actions (action\_option\_batch) in the current state, while the critic is just about evaluating the Q-value (critic\_batch) in the static graphs. We could try to merge these two, but that would be very complex (e.g. had to separate the action\_option\_batch and critic\_batch, by applying operators to it).

At this time, I am not merging these two (critic and actor
