[108/200] Running: What are residual connections in transformers?...

🧠 LLM Response:


In transformers, residual connections are shortcuts added around each sub-layer, between the input and output of each sub-layer, allowing the model to skip over layers if necessary. This helps to prevent the problem of vanishing gradients, where the gradients of the loss function become too small to effectively train the model. By allowing the model to skip layers, residual connections enable the model to learn deeper representations more easily.

Residual connections were first introduced in the context of deep neural networks by He et al. in their paper "Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition." In this paper, the authors showed that adding residual connections to a deep neural network significantly improved its performance, allowing it to learn much deeper representations than would otherwise be possible.

In transformers, residual connections are used in both the encoder and decoder. In the encoder, residual connections are added around each of the multi-head self-attention and feed-forward sub-layers. In the decoder, residual connections are added around the multi-head self-attention, encoder-decoder attention, and feed-forward sub-layers.

Residual connections are implemented using the addition operator, which allows the input of a sub-layer to be added to its output. This is done by adding a special "add" operation to the transformer architecture, which takes the input of a sub-layer and the output of the subsequent sub-layer, and adds them together. The resulting sum is then passed through a normalization layer, such as layer normalization or batch normalization, before being fed into the next sub-layer.

Overall, residual connections are an important component of transformer architectures, enabling them to learn deeper representations and avoid the problem of vanishing gradients. They are a simple and effective way to improve the performance of deep neural networks, and are widely used in a variety of applications, including natural language processing, computer vision, and speech recognition.
