A: Checkpoints improve fault tolerance by periodically saving a stable copy of a program's memory, allowing it to be restored in case of a failure. Checkpoints can help ensure that data is not lost due to crashes or other errors, and can also help reduce the time required to recover from a fault. By saving a checkpoint, a program can quickly restore its state to the point of the last checkpoint, rather than having to recompute all of the work that was done since the program started. This can help improve the overall reliability and efficiency of a system. Additionally, checkpoints can be used as a way to coordinate the execution of distributed applications, allowing them to continue running even if some of the nodes in the system fail.
