🧠 LLM Response:


Token-wise parallelism is a built-in technique in Hugging Face’s Transformers library to accelerate the most common, dense attention matrix operations. It does so by aggressively removing memory redundancies that already exist in a self-attention mechanism.

The name token-wise refers to the fact that the algorithm is efficient because it only has to store the Q (queries), K (keys), V (values), and attn_mask for each input token, and for each layer of the transformer. A dependence on these four parameters restrict the attention computation, and it allows us to not store intermediary attention matrices in memory.

By removing the need to store intermediary attention matrices, we significantly reduce the GPU memory usage during dense attention operations. This is especially relevant for GPU architectures that cannot fit single large dense matrices in their limited memory, such as the NVIDIA A100.

