[116/200] Running: How do shared embeddings help in decoder-only models?...

🧠 LLM Response:

In a variant of a decoder-only model, namely in the backbone of a generative infilling model, we learn a subword-level discrete variational autoencoder (discrete-VAE). The discrete variables, ops, decomposed as in a mixture-of-experts (MoE) and chosen based on the token position, are not fed through more layers. Instead, we explicitly learn shared embeddings for the context, the generation target, and the ops. Because of the MoE-based decompositions, a fast decoder can be implementedwhere the ops that lead to the most context-conditioned likely outputs can be quickly found by first enforcing that the token and the ops embeddings are in the \_same \_embedding space and then selecting the top-kops based on their dot-products with the context embedding space. (The 'similarity\_selector' mentioned at: <https://github.com/salesforce/LAVA/issues/344> is being used for this) This leads to a multi-faceted loss function, with the shared embeddings being the main building block.
