[118/200] Running: What is the purpose of dropout during training?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 Can you please describe how this works and provide instance during training?

Dropout is an algorithmic regularization method used during training to prevent—or at least reduce—overfitting.

To understand how it works, imagine a common situation where you train a neural network using a dataset of limited size. The network is typically less sophisticated than the real-world problems for which you can use it. Yet you still want this network to make accurate predictions even when fed new, unseen data, and to handle situations that it wasn’t trained with.

To achieve this, during training, you randomly and temporarily remove some neurons from the network in what is known as dropout. You reduce the set of active neurons by 50% on average. By removing neurons, you create a new sparse network. While this sparse network still makes predictions based on existing neurons, it thinks about two or three steps ahead and expects that some of its predictions can change due to a missing part of the network. The result is that this sparse network making fewer errors is better prepared for a more non-deterministic neural network when the removed neurons are included again.

During training, the network naturally changes and becomes more accurate based on various loss minimization techniques—such as gradient descent—and its suggestions become stronger. Yet you don’t want an accurate network to be overconfident. Contextually, by preventing strong suggestions from the network, you make it more secure and robust.

This is why you should use dropout during training.

I hope this answers your question. Let me know if you have any more.
[119/200] Running: What is the role of GELU activation in transformers?...

🧠 LLM Response:


The GELU (Gaussian Error Linear Units) activation function is used in the transformer architecture to introduce non-linearity into the model. It is an alternative to the more commonly used ReLU (Rectified Linear Unit) activation function.

In a transformer, the GELU activation function is typically used in the feed-forward neural network (FFNN) that is applied to each position in the input sequence independently. The FFNN is composed of two linear layers with a GELU activation function between them, and it is applied after the self-attention mechanism in each transformer block.

The GELU activation function is defined as:

GELU(x) = 0.5x(1 + tanh[√(2/π)(x + 0.044715x²)])

This activation function is a smooth approximation of the ReLU activation function. Unlike ReLU, which is linear for positive inputs and zero for negative inputs, GELU has a gradual transition from negative to positive values. This makes it more robust to the vanishing gradient problem, which can occur when the input to a neuron is too small, leading to a very small gradient and slow convergence during training.

The GELU activation function has been shown to be effective in transformer architectures and has been used in models such as BERT (Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers) and RoBERTa (Robustly Optimized BERT Pretraining Approach). The use of GELU in these models has been credited with improving their performance compared to models that use ReLU activation.

In summary, the GELU activation function is used in transformer models to introduce non-linearity into the feed-forward neural networks that are applied after the self-attention mechanism. It is a smooth approximation of the ReLU activation function that is more robust to the vanishing gradient problem and has been shown to improve the performance of transformer models.
[120/200] Running: Explain forward and backward pass in transformers....

