In the context of the Transformer architecture, the forward pass refers to the process of passing input vectors through the network to produce output vectors. This involves several steps, including embedding the input vectors, passing them through the encoder and decoder layers, and applying the final output layer.

The backward pass, also known as backpropagation, is the process of computing the gradients of the model parameters with respect to the loss function, and updating the parameters using an optimization algorithm, such as stochastic gradient descent. This allows the model to learn from its errors and improve its performance on future input vectors.

The forward pass and backward pass are performed alternately during training, with the forward pass used to compute the model's predictions and the backward pass used to update the model's parameters. During inference, only the forward pass is necessary to produce output vectors for new input vectors.
