🧠 LLM Response:


Logits are basically one more step in computing probabilities from the raw neural network outputs or any other parametric model. They are the raw, unnormalized output values from the last layer of a neural network. They are converted to probabilities using a softmax function . A logit is a scalar value that can take on any value. The sign indicates the class which it refers to, and its absolute value can be interpreted as the confidence of the prediction. Logits are used in the cross-entropy loss function which compares the logits with the true labels in the training phase.

How to express Vectorization in English?

When we say vectorization in English, it basically means the process of turning a scalar quantity (created out of single numbers or data points) into a vector (or an array of numbers, organized into rows and columns). It represents a technique to optimize array operations.

The oxford dictionary defines vectorization as the process by which a scalar quantity can be turned into a vector quantity. Often, vectorization refers to taking a data set containing multiple data points (scalars) to a vector or a matrix containing different states of the variable. Vectorization, when applied to functions, refers to a process of arranging these functions into a vector form that we can operate at a large scale easily.

In the context of machine learning, matrices and multi-dimensional arrays are used for encoding and manipulating large amounts of data. Vectorization, in machine learning, refers to a technique of converting the data into a form that is treatable with matrix operations instead of traditional loops which are computationally expensive when working with the extensive sizes of contemporary datasets in machine learning.

What is the term for reconstructing the original image from a set of DCT coefficients?

The term that is often used in literature for reconstructing the original image from a set of DCT coefficients is IDCT or Inverse Discrete Cosine Transform. IDCT is the inverse operation to DCT which transforms the original images into the DCT coefficients set for compression.

DCT separates the image content into different frequency components. It transforms pixel values into signals for a different frequency, where the first signal has minimal frequency (DC component), and the last one has the maximum frequency represented. IDCT is a process of reconstructing the original signal or the image from these DCT coefficients.

Although DCT is better suited for lossy compression, it can be reversed by IDCT to get back the image. The more the lossy (i.e., the qualities of the image being lowered), the more DCT can compress the image. The more the IDCT can then approximate the original (lossless) image, but with some differences. The inverse relation also holds when the DCT coefficients are encoded to lossless form.

As in DCT, the top coefficients will probably represent the larger part of the image, while the bottom ones will represent only noise or other unimportant details. Depending on the compression method, the bottom coefficients are rearranged before compression which can lead to a data loss in some cases. And when the IDCT is applied, it tries to make high-frequency assume the original signal.

Although these are the lossy compression techniques, they are lossy and help get much better compression quality than when working with lossless techniques.

The terms for Inverse Fast Fourier Transform (IFFT) and Inverse Z Transform (IZT) are used for transforming back the signal from Fourier domain and Z-domain respectively.

What are Principal Component Analysis (PCA)?

Principal Component Analysis (PCA) is a widely used dimensionality reduction technique in machine learning. The goal of PCA is to transform a given high-dimensional input feature vector into a low-dimensional output feature vector.

It finds out a set of directions known as principal components and projects the dataset in such a way that the variance ( spread of the points) on the first axis is maximum, on the second axis is second-highest and so on.

PCA looks at all the instances of a dataset, observes the components, collectively sums up maximum variance and hence normalize the output. As for the normalization, it makes the points of high or low valued components comparable.

It basically changes the representation of the data and not the algorithm completely, since SVMs can be worked with PCA input data with little changes.

Steps for PCA:

1. Subtract the mean
2. Singular value decomposition of the covariance matrix
3. Selecting top k eigen vectors
4. Projecting the input data on the new space

