## Answer (1)

For one model type, distilbert, you can create the JSON config file by using the below (or similar) code that extracts the configuration of the to-be-distilled version of bert

```
baseModel = AutoModel.from_pretrained("bert-base-uncased")
config = AutoConfig.from_model(baseModel, arch_type="distilledbert")
config.to_json_file("distilledbert-config.json")
```

For other model types you will not find this easy translation but you can re purposes existing configs and change some of the elements to adjust it to your model.

## Answer (0)

The `ModelConfig JSON` is a convenient way to load model and fine-tuning configurations.

The JSON files are available here and come with every (pretrained) model which can be loaded using the Transformers library.

On the other hand, when you want to train your own model, you can define the configuration both when saving and loading the `config.json` file.

========================================

**1. Save config.json upon training**

`model.config.to_json_file("./config.json")` // saves the configuration of the model upon training

Note: This can be particularly useful if you have changed any parameter, e.g., `learning_rate`, during fine-tuning.

**2. Load config.json during fine-tuning**

`config = AutoConfig.from_pretrained("./config.json")`

**3. Create a new JSON file based on the JSON file which comes with a model**

`DistilBertConfig = DistilBertConfig.from_json_file("./bert-base-uncased/config.json")`

Note: You can edit following section in the JSON file (`n_layer`) to access the exact model.

```
 "architectures" : [
  "DistilBertForSequenceClassification",
  "DistilBertForQuestionAnswering",
  "DistilBertForPreTraining",
  "DistilBertModel"
],
"n_layers":6
```

========================================

The named tuple’s fields are the original arguments’s field name passed to the constructor.

**Example** : AutoDistilBertConfig.FILE_NAME

========================================

I recommend reading this thread if you want to learn more about the `auto_class` file (`XXXAutoConfig.py`) itself without reading the source code.

