Hey Evan! Awesome that you are logging your metrics in a CSV file First of all: Your example looks pretty good 😊 The column types are the same for all rows, so the struct tag `,type:"float64"` just has to be placed on the first value in every CSVValue-Type I would just mention some minor points to consider:

1. Use `os.Create` instead of `os.OpenFile` to create a new CSV file. This will overwrite an existing file, and if the file does not exist it will be created. To append to an existing CSV file, you can `os.OpenFile` the append mode.
2. The last property of the struct is often not used by spreadsheet libraries like LibreOffice and Google Sheets. It's best to use `string` for this property.
3. Your column headers may not be used as actual values in some cases. It's therefore best to not have the same name as a chosen column value. Once again, this is important if you want to use the CSV with other software.
4. Instead of `struct`, sometimes `[]string` is used to specify a CSV value. This works because a single struct is converted to a string array with `f`, and `[]string` directly converts to separate CSV values. The only drawback with the array approach is that you have to remember to set the column headers with `CSVHeader` and not the struct values with `f.Write`.
Original '3.5:':

I it's recommend to use the `csv.Reader` and specifically the `CSVHeader` method to read the header of a CSV file. You are currently doing this through `bufio` first. For a big CSV file with many columns, the `CSVHeader` is more memory efficient because it only reads the first line. Check out the docs for more info.

