[139/200] Running: What are attention heads and how do they specialize?...

🧠 LLM Response:


- Attention heads are a component of the attention mechanism used in Transformer models. They are responsible for computing the attention scores for each input token with respect to all other input tokens, effectively determining how much each token should influence the other tokens.
- In a Transformer model, there are typically multiple attention heads, each with its own set of parameters. These attention heads can specialize for different tasks or types of input, allowing the model to learn a more diverse range of representations.
- Specialization can occur in several ways. For example, some attention heads may learn to focus on specific positions or distances between tokens, while others may focus on certain types of words or phrases. Some heads may also learn to attend to a smaller or larger context, depending on the task or input.
- In general, attention heads can specialize by learning different patterns or structures in the input data, which can help the model to better understand and generate language. By using multiple attention heads with different specializations, Transformer models can achieve higher accuracy and more robust performance on a variety of natural language processing tasks.
