[141/200] Running: What are model shards and when are they used?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Model shards are pieces—or shards—of an AI model. They are used in a technique known as distributed training. However, a question arises: Why could we want to distribute training of our model?

We distribute the training of our model when we need to work with large models, and consequently, large datasets. Imagine for a moment a large model. The word large here is relative, but it could refer to about 10GB in its more modest basal deployment. You come to this situation when you want to implement a large model to extract deep semantic information. However, large models have one severe problem:memory constraints.

So why don’t we deploy this large model on a powerful computer? Let’s imagine that our computational unit is a single standard GPU with 11 GB or 8GB of VRAM. It’s easy to see that our first candidate is computationally unfeasible. Our second candidate is a better option: it has a decent computational capability, but it’s still unfeasible because our data will not fit in its VRAM.

To overcome this problem, one easy way is to split our model into smaller parts—called shards—and train our model distributively through a technique called distributed training. Distributed training is used to divide the training workload of a single model over many computing nodes. In our case, this computation translates into optimizing our large computational graph over many GPUs, letting us bypass memory limitations.

That’s when model shards become useful: to train a large model in a distributed way:

> Distributed training involves training a large model on a ’cluster’ of computing resources called workers. There are a number of distributed strategies, and TensorFlow supports several:
>
> If the model is too large to fit in memory on each device, consider using model parallelism.
>
> If you have a very large dataset that can’t fit in memory, but model parallelism can fit, consider using data parallelism.

Which technique is used depends on the problem you want to solve in different ways:

- Model parallelism: This technique is better suited for when you have a large complex model that you want to train, and you want to divide this model into smaller networks that are easily handled by each machine of the cluster in which you distribute the training. It’s important to note that each parallel model section usually utilizes one GPU.
- Data parallelism: This technique is better suited for cases when you want to train a large dataset through shards, where each shard is distributed through several GPUs in the cluster. Each shard is distributed over various GPUs, thus allowing to quickly parallelize the process, and converge faster to our target goal.

In both cases, we end up with a better utilization of computational resources, which in this context are GPUs.

It’s important to note that using distributed training could be seen as a Pandora’s box: before you try to implement it, you should be aware that some challenges are involved. One of these challenges is that distributed training is a complex problem that should be adequately modeled beforehand, both for regularizing its complexity and for visualizing the points of failure that could threaten your implementation during production (and even the implementation itself).

In this article I want to show you how to implement a distributed training in an easy way, taking into account our architectonical constraints. We will start using the TensorFlow queues API as well as the LSTM cells to create a model that generates music through LSTM cells. After that, we will see how to parallelize our model using model and data parallelism.

Let’s start with understanding the architecture.

