[143/200] Running: What is batch inference and why is it faster?...

🧠 LLM Response:
}\author{Chris Lott}
\date{}
\maketitle

\section{What is batch inference?}

Batch inference is the process of making predictions on a large number of inputs at once, as opposed to making predictions on one input at a time. This is done by processing multiple inputs simultaneously in a batch, taking advantage of the parallel processing capabilities of modern hardware.

For example, if you have a machine learning model that predicts the sentiment of a given text, you can use batch inference to predict the sentiment of multiple texts at once. Instead of passing one text to the model at a time, you would pass a list or array of texts, and the model would return a list or array of predictions.

\section{Why is batch inference faster?}

Batch inference is faster because it allows you to take full advantage of the parallel processing capabilities of modern hardware, such as CPUs and GPUs. These processors are designed to perform many operations at the same time, and by processing multiple inputs simultaneously in a batch, you can maximize the utilization of these processors and reduce the overall time required to make predictions.

For example, consider a CPU with four cores. If you use batch inference to process four inputs at once, each core can work on a separate input, allowing the CPU to complete the task four times faster than if you had processed the inputs one at a time.

Similarly, many modern GPUs are designed to perform thousands or even millions of operations in parallel. By using batch inference to process large batches of inputs, you can take full advantage of the parallel processing capabilities of these GPUs and make predictions much faster than would be possible with serial inference.

In summary, batch inference is faster than serial inference because it allows you to take full advantage of the parallel processing capabilities of modern hardware, reducing the overall time required to make predictions.
