[149/200] Running: How to parse streaming responses from vLLM?...

🧠 LLM Response:

-------------------------------------------

### Parsing with built in support for JSON

By default vLLM attempts to parse JSON responses before providing the result.  In most
cases this is what you want to happen automatically.

### Parsing JSON manually

You can disable the built in JSON parsing support to handle JSON parsing yourself.  You
might want to disable it if you have several different response categories that you
want to handle differently than the default serialization library.  In that case you
might want to simply turn the JSON parsing off with:

```python
from vllm import Client

c = Client(auto_parse=False)
```

Even if you have turned off the automatic parsing you can still request JSON at any
specific time with:

```python
parsed = c.parse_response(JSON, raw_response=response.json())
```

### Custom parsing

If you want to do custom parsing of responses using vLLM, you can rewrite the `Client.parse`
method of your `Client` instance.  This should use a
[class that implements `ResponseSerializer`](https://github.com/OlexiyKot/vLLM/blob/master/vllm/serializers.py),
such as the `JSONResponseSerializer`.  If you want to make defaults per-client (you
have a client with one data processing pipeline and you often reuse `Client` instances
for different profiles with different expected data formats), you can [create a new
sub-class of `Client`](https://github.com/OlexiyKot/vllm/blob/239d4755d7d61749bc9a7738a12478fe3a3dfe18/vllm/client.py#L34)
that will override the default `CLI_SERIALIZER` with one that you prefer.

### Custom parsing with official views

If you want to use custom views, please refer to the [separate documentation for views](views.md).

Note that according to yesterday's words of [Greg Brown, "views are important feature for
reducing overhead associated with parsing and enforcing user's expectations about
the format of LLM vLLM responses".](https://twitter.com/gregvonree/status/1500407760477915138)  So
please use views whenever they're available and provide less cluttered experience to all users.
[150/200] Running: How does deep caching work in inference?...

🧠 LLM Response:


In order to reduce inference latency, deep caching is one of MLCube’s supported caching techniques. It creates a baseline performance model and caches layers of convolutional neural networks to minimize inference latency. Once the first request to the RunMLCube funtion is finished, the cached copy of the model can be reused for several other calls to the same function. Thus, deep caching considerably enhances inference performance for many users executing the same machine learning app, basically since the time-consuming model loading phase can be skipped.

To enable deep caching, you can activate it through the `store_model_cache` parameter in the model preview URL. The huge cache file can be stored in a cloud storage that is accessible to all the users, or stored locally as a tarball that can be uploaded manually to assist in performance improvement.

Thus, deep caching is beneficial for inference scenarios where the same machine learning model is repeatedly executed with similar inputs, without the need for adaptive retraining. It provides an easy and efficient way to enhance inference performance, leading to reduced latencies and faster response times.
