[154/200] Running: What is a chat template in HuggingFace?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Hugging Face chat templates are pre-built chat interfaces for generating responses to a user input. The templates contain two parts: a frontend that renders the UI, and a backend that specifies how the model should be used to generate responses.

The templates make it easy for users to customize the look and feel of the chat interface, as well as to integrate the chat with different languages, modalities and models. Hugging Face provides several pre-built templates that can be used with a wide variety of models.

For example, the `chat-html` template is a minimal chat interface implemented using HTML, CSS and JavaScript. It allows users to provide a model with an input message and displays the model's response in the chat window. Other templates include `chat-local`, `chat-streamlit` and `chat-voicemail`.
[155/200] Running: What is inference latency and why is it important?...

🧠 LLM Response:


In the AI/ML space, inference begins once the trained model receives some input. After the model receives the input (i.e., image, video frame, speech frame, text, etc.), the inference process involves model processing – which is essentially computations required to make a prediction on the input - and returns the prediction.

Inference latency, or inference time, is the time it takes to perform the inference. Inference latency is used to reflect the speed the model, or inference system, makes predictions, and is critical for many real-time applications.

For example, if you are building a lip-reading system for a live broadcast setup, you really need that system to process speech in real time. In this case, inference latency could be as important as or even more important than the accuracy of the system, since lip reading with several-second delay is not useful.

Inference latency can vary across different applications based on the complexity of the model, input size, computational resources, etc. Following is a table showing latency on Raspberry Pi 3B (Dual-core 1.3GHz Cortex-A53 + 1GB RAM), for various models, using TensorFlow Lite:

An actual system always involves several other components in addition to ML models. In these cases, it is reasonable to assume 20-30 ms of additional latency due to network transmissions and other processing delays.

With that being said, minimizing inference latency is critical for real-time applications. Apple designed the Neural Engine in A11 for a good reason - short inference latency to be used in many real-time applications. NVIDIA ships top-end GPUs with TensorRT because of short latency and high performance benefits. Google designed TensorFlow Lite for the exact same reasons. FPGAs and ASICs all follow the same trend.

Inference latency is extremely important for real-time applications. Decreasing latency is one way to improve real-time performance.

How about accuracy?

Accuracy is commonly measured by top1 and top5. Top1 accuracy indicates whether or not the highest ranked class label matches the ground truth. Top5 accuracy indicates whether or not the ground truth label is present somewhere in top 5 predictions.

For example, if you build a patient diagnosis system with medical image dataset, and the output model classifies whether or not the patient has stage 1 hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and the only ground truth label is “0”, or normal. In this case, top1 and top5 accuracy look very similar, since the output is binary:

`Model Output:`

`-------`

`hypertrophic cardiomyopathy`

`diabetes`

`common cold`

`-------`

`0.95`

`0.02`

`0.03`

For a model with large number of output classes with multiple modalities, more sophisticated ways of measuring inference accuracy are necessary.

Why does inference accuracy vary?

Inference accuracy can vary for two main reasons:

1. Model and dataset discrepancy: ML models are built on top of learning algorithms, which react based on distribution of inputs dataset they have been trained on. It is straightforward to imagine that inference accuracy changes based on the distribution of input data for the intended application.
2. Model pruning and compression: To optimize speed and memory consumption, various model pruning, distilling or compression methods can be used, all of which may impact accuracy.

Why does inference latency vary?

Inference latency increases for several reasons:

1. Model complexity: Larger and more complicated models tend to increase inference latency.
2. Input size: Larger input size increases inference latency.

These are overly general reasons, but the differences in inference latency among different edge devices, as shown in the table below can be astonishing. Note that the difference is from ARM Cortex-A53 CPUs and the last ARM Cortex-A73 CPU currently available in mobile:

The difference in latency is primarily due to the different optimizations on different hardware, as well as computational resources available for the AI/ML tasks at hand.

Minimizing inference latency is an important topic, especially for applications that require real time decision-making. Hygon version of Zen-based CPUs competes for this exact reason (TensorFlow ported to AMD’s native instruction set promises to
