🧠 LLM Response:

==============================================

So you spent several hours training your large model, and now you want to use and share it. However, you rediscover that it takes quite some memory to load a model checkpoint.
Let me introduce you to some techniques to reduce your models memory footprint while staying mostly performant.

Index
----

-   Introduction
-   Using mixed-precision
-   Reducing the number of heads
-   Reducing model dimensionality
-   Comparision

Introduction
------------

All credits to the underlying work go to the great \dalle.pics,\
who wrote and published [this](https://dalle.pics/\~DALL-E/post/shrinking-llama-memory-footprint) here on the Hugging Face Blog.

Reducing models memory footprint - How this is even possible?
-------------------------------------------------------------

You might ask yourself the question about what the rationale behind reducing the memory footprint is.
While some tricks do not reduce performance too much, some might lead to a severe performance decrease.
I am going to list three considerations behind this work that will give you more context.

1.  The question to: **How long** will I use a single model for inference?
    -   If you want to run a single model for days or weeks without the need to load different models, you might want to allocate more memory upfront to avoid having to constantly restart the model.
2.  How much **data** will I send to this model?
    -   Some of the learning-based model pretrained on large datasets cache information internally.
    This allows the model to sometimes improve inference quality while not requiring additional forward passes.
    By making the model more memory efficient without sacrificing quality, we can also allow for larger batches.
3.  **Speed**: How fast do I need to be?
    -   Less memory does not always mean less performance. There are different ways to design models so that less memory footprint leads to faster speeds.
    \
    **Pros**

    -    You will be able to load and evaluate models more quickly.
    This saves time and increases experiment sampling and iteration time during inference.
    -    You can use larger batch sizes for the same amount of memory, potentially improving model performance.

    **Cons**

    -    Model redesign might potentially worsens the model performance.
    This happens if you remove or reduce critical parts of the models.

Using mixed-precision
-------------------

Mixed-precision refers to hybrid float16 and float32 training that allows you to use lower-precision floating-point data types, which in turn require fewer memory resources and achieve higher throughput.
\
LLaMA models support a uniform precision setting and allows FP32 and FP16.
This is controlled with the `llama.config.ModelConfig` classes `fp16` setting.
Setting this to `True` will automatically enable mixed precision.

Reducing the number of heads
----------------------------

Another way to reduce the memory footprint would be to reduce the number of heads.
Heads are parallel tasks in Transformer models.
Accordingly, a reduced number of heads will lead to less parallelism. However, this has to be traded against model degradation.
Additionally, it can have a significant positive impact.

Reducing model dimensionality
------------------------------

You could also reduce the dimensionality of your model.
One idea here was to reduce the hidden size (`d_model`) of the LLaMA models.

Comparision
-----------

![Comparision of different model settings. ("h=16" refers to the `head_num` and ""d=128" represents the `hidden_size` in the LLaMA model config.)](https://miro.medium.com/max/2000/1*-ySmVHkPviD9_V_ft5oRbg.png)
While the number of parameters decreased only slightly (`h=16,d=128` -> 2.72G), the inference speed improved by 10\% (see \[12\] for workload details).
Also, the memory footprint shrank considerably by ~10.7 GB or ~44\%.

All of these techniques provide excellent starting points to reduce memory footprints.
The resulting model, of course, will still be slower and less capable compared to the
