I need to get a set of JSON tokens from Azure key vault using curl. However, there are too many JSON values (>3000) to be returned as a single JSON value due to Azure response size limitation. I’ve no problem in parsing the JSON strings as they are returned individually, but I do have trouble handling a stream of JSON strings – After my client receives a stream of JSON strings, how can I combine them all into a single big JSON string to be parsed as single JSON document?

Is this approach to stream JSON tokens in a specified depth supported by Azure rest api?
This approach is the only way I can get tokens, no matter whether Azure support it or not. Even I stream JSON tokens on client-side script, libraries such as jQuery.parseJSON only accept a single JSON string as valid input.

## Solution 1

Each `JSON` object can be read and parsed by itself.
You would only parse a full object. Note that since http is stream based it is unlikely you will read multiple full objects at a time anyway!

```
// read by "chunk"
//
while(z = readchunk())
   parse(z)

function readchunk()
{
  // read as much data as possible from input.
  // returns "", EOF
}

function parse(z)
{
   var d = JSON.parse(z);
   // do something with `d`
}
```

This is the approach I would use for `feeds` which may be processed by stream like XML feeds of the past. I would be recommend reading the JSON into an AST somehow(https://json.org/jsonorg.js is a good lightweight library for this) and parse the object from the data.

However you should explain why you are passing around multiple JSON objects and a link to the library you are using to parse the JSON to see if those are worth while options.

For example bluebird makes it easy to read a stream bound to a request in a loop

```
var stream = Promise.promisifyAll(request.get("..."))
    , z

// make request
//
while(true) {

  // awaits a chunk at a time from the stream.
  var z = await stream.next();
  if (!z) // end of stream
    return;
  // parse the z

  reactDom.render(<MyJSONobject data={z} />, document.getElementById('stuff'));

}
```

## Solution 2

For streaming a JSON object in chunks, the streaming-json-parser library could be helpful, see https://www.npmjs.com/package/streaming-json-parser. The JSON parser recovers from reading an incorrect part of a JSON object and only fails if the end of the stream is reached while parsing. Below is an example using Axios to stream large JSON objects from an API endpoint and parse them using streaming-json-parser, in Node.js:

```
import axios from "axios";
import parse from "streaming-json-parser";

// URL of API endpoint returning JSON object in chunks
const url = "https://example.com/api/objects/stream";

const  parser = parse();

// open request to the API endpoint
const request = axios({
    url,
    method: "get",
    responseType: "stream",
    maxContentLength: Infinity,
    validateStatus: () => true
});

// read data from request
request.data.on("data", chunk => {

    // parse the chunk and print the parsed object
    parser.write(chunk).then(json => console.log(json));
});

// if all data is read, end the parser
request.data.on("end", () => parser.end());
```

