[168/200] Running: Explain streaming benchmarks with high concurrency....

🧠 LLM Response:


When your application has any type of concurrency -- that is, multiple threads or requests accessing the same data set at the same time -- how well your data streams perform can make a big difference. This performance is often not well explained, leaving streams benchmarks difficult to interpret.

Rates and throughput are common benchmarks for streams, but they do not necessarily tell the whole story. For example, you may have a stream operation that is efficient in isolation, where nothing else is happening. Yet when two concurrent operations try to access the stream at the same time, severe performance degradation may occur, due to contention for shared resources.

There are a variety of causes of this contention. Contention can occur when data is protected by a lock, and one thread (or request) must wait for the lock to become available so that it can continue. Contention can also occur due to resource limits of the underlying hardware, when more than a certain number of threads try to access a resource (for example, the CPU or a network interface), causing threads to wait in a queue for the resource to become available.

To get a clearer picture of stream operations under high concurrency, something beyond just the raw performance rate of the stream operation is required. The streams benchmark must include not only the performance of the stream operation, but also details on how the performance changes in the presence of one or more streams operating concurrently.

Specifically, the benchmark should define how many streams are operating, and what happens when the number is increased. This helps differentiate between data streams that parallelize well, and those that do not.

Assuming that the benchmark demonstrates that the streams parallelize well, then the benchmark should include information about how the performance scales as additional streams are added. Scaling refers to the performance gained as more streams are initiated. For example, if using multiple streams causes the performance to be twice as high as a single stream, then the streams have a linear scale. If the performance does not double when adding one more thread, then the streams have poor scaling, indicating that the streams are performing poorly in the context of high concurrency. Knowing this information is vital when considering streams for use cases with high concurrency.
[169/200] Running: How to calculate power usage per prompt?...

🧠 LLM Response:


There is no straightforward way to calculate per prompt power usage. However, if you closely monitor the system, you can evaluate the power usage when the application is running and not running. Then, you can estimate the power consumption per prompt by dividing the difference by the number of prompts when the application is in an active state.

Here's a way you can estimate it:

1. Monitor the power consumption of your device without the application running. Make sure no other applications or background processes are using significant power during this period.
2. Now, run the application that shows prompts and monitor the power consumption again.
3. Count the number of prompts shown during the active period of the application.
4. Subtract the idle power consumption from the active power consumption.
5. Divide the difference by the number of prompts to get the approximate power usage per prompt.

Keep in mind that this is an estimation and actual power consumption may vary depending on factors like prompt format, device type, and other power-consuming processes running simultaneously. Additionally, power consumption will not be linearly related to the number of prompts, as the overhead of starting and stopping the application will also have an impact on power usage.
