 Here is a simple study: amzon SageMaker uses the YAML called model quantiles to pack 1000’s of ML models and just-in-time scale maching learning inference with any data source or framework anywhere.

When building an Amazon SageMaker model, you can specify a number of quantiles for which to include models. This allows the model to scale by selecting the model with the best possible latency for the actual data it receives, balancing precision with service response times for a truly “just-in-time” inference engine.

Below is a chart showing how latency changes as you change the number of included quantiles. For this experiment, I wrote a simple script that usesGensim to create word embeddings with varying sizes on a Titan X Pascal GPU.

These models are evaluated on the same machine with warm caches, so it’s a direct performance comparison. When the final model is selected from the quantiles, the total inference latency is the addition of the model loading/prep time plus the prediction time. As the models get larger, there is a trade-off between the two constituent latencies. The model loading time grows with the model volume, while the prediction time remains relatively constant.

Even though these models are significantly larger than what you’d normally expect for deep learning (the 100-quantile model has a ~50GB binary), as with many things in machine learning, the choice of how much to scale is a trade-off.
