A little while ago, I was faced with the challenge of debugging why one particular streaming endpoint for Redis JSON was experiencing locked responses--users would follow a link, load the page, and be left staring at the server’s `Loading...` indicator for 10 or 15 seconds before everything rendered for them.

Because I’m super meticulous with my logging (especially when I implement a new endpoint), I didn’t see the classic PHP fatal error type thing, which means the overall application itself wasn’t throwing errors at all.

This presented a challenging situation: how do you debug something that hasn’t actually fallen over?

It would’ve been easy to throw the request into a Chrome Network Traffic recording, but something told me that this wasn’t the low-hanging fruit I needed to pick.

I thought out of the box, I need to somehow capture an IO profile of these responses. A non-blocking profiler wouldn’t work either, so what did I do? I created a pretty much custom logging profile in `glpi_profiling` which provied me with the information I needed.

You can see where the program’s time is spent!

And importantly, because I happened to know that I didn’t do anything much with preflighting authentication, I was able to isolate the issue really quickly to individual queries in the Redis JSON library–I hadn’t properly handled the situation of iterating unlocked responses in the context of the redis async client, and because of this small issue in handling async streams, everything blocke!

I would’ve been typing this log information out by hand if it wasn’t for the sheer ease and delight of leveraging the incredible `glpi_profiling` class in GLPI, a lead developer at GLPI left a more in-depth example of its use here!

