This is a very unwise approach to starting a generator that does not start.

A generator needs to be connected to a ground with a grounding rod.  Check that your generator does not ground to the frame (house ground).

If you really must hotwire it and you can't get it started any other way, try this:

Open the cowling or cover and remove the spark plugs.  Unplug the spark plug harnesses from their small spark plugs while leaving the wires to the spark plugs attached.

Then try cranking the engine with the spark plugs removed.

If there is resistance in the starter circuit, you may have to remove the starter motor.  Disconnect the wire from the starter motor.  Crank it again.

You need a literal "short" in the diagnostic test.  If it doesn't just start without a battery, something (maybe the wiring) may be damaged in the generator starter circuit or the windings.  Have a professional look at it.

