[176/200] Running: What is an OpenAI-compatible inference API?...

🧠 LLM Response:

--------------------------------------------

An OpenAI-compatible inference interface is a server that listens to requests coming from a client,
 and proceeds to generate text outputs based on the given request. Such a service **MUST NOT** use
 the OpenAI API; instead, it should be based on a different language model, which is not subject
 to the same licensing constraints as the official OpenAI API.

There are no requirements on the served model head or ensembles used, beyond generation of high-
quality, pleasant text.

This definition follows that of [Sergei Romanenko](https://twitter.com/cryptexorama/status/1580471817043597312),
promoted by [@crazyaboutai](https://twitter.com/crazyaboutai) on twitter.

Serve an OpenAI compatible inference model using LitMod
-----------------------------------------------------

LitMod is a lit-jit based model server. It allows you to easily serve your fine tuned model in minutes.

### Comparison of Serde and LitMod

| | Serde | LitMod |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Process scheduling | Built-in multiprocess server, can be run with `docker compose` | User-defined process scheduling (Python [multiprocessing.Pool](https://docs.python.org/3/library/multiprocessing.html#module-multiprocessing.pool) implementation) |
| JS execution supported | N | Y |
| Serves RobustFinetuned models illiterally | N | Y |

### Instillation

To install LitMod, clone our [GitHub repository](https://github.com/trending/gitstar-ranking), or
install using pip:

```
pip install litmod
```

Make sure you have defined your environment variable for model path before serving:

```
export LITMOD_MODEL_PATH=path/to/your/robust/fine-tuned/model
```

### Server setup

We use `fast-api` to serve the requests. To start a single process server, execute:

```
litmod serve
```

For improved performance on modern CPUs, we recommend to use `docker` to run a multprocess server.

Below example is ready to go. You can also configure it up with any other docker based server that
is suited for the task.

#### Docker Compose setup

In the docker compose file below, we deploy N processes, with each going to its `jupyternotebook`
container. We can scale this setup horizontally to M instances to improve throughput.
Each notebook worker runs our `litmod` script, which acts as our services starting point
and implementation of the FastAPI servlet, as well as our queue, and process management.

The following `docker-compose.yml` file illustrates environment setup involving 8 workers.

> **Multi-process**

```yaml
version: '3'

services:
  worker_0:
    image: jupyter/notebook:latest
    command: ["litmod", "serve", "0", "-nodisplay"]  # Start the worker on process when the container starts
    volumes:
      - "./config.py:/app/config.py"  # Mount an adjustable proportions of model instances in memory
      - "./log.yaml:/app/log.yaml"
      - "./soa-model:/app/soa-model"  # Mount the entire model in the worker container
    environment:
      MODEL_PATH: $LITMOD_MODEL_PATH  # Mount the path in the container

  worker_1:
    image: jupyter/notebook:latest
    command: ["litmod", "serve", "1", "-nodisplay"]
    volumes:
      - "./config.py:/app/config.py"
      - "./log.yaml:/app/log.yaml"
      - "./soa-model:/app/soa-model"
    environment:
      MODEL_PATH: $LITMOD_MODEL_PATH

  worker_2:
    image: jupyter/notebook:latest
    command:
[177/200] Running: How to handle decoding errors in stream?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Decoding errors in a steam usually mean that there is a bug in the encoder (that is using the stream to generate decodable data), a bug in the decoder or that the decoder encoutered man-made data. It's hard to give generic advice on that. Here are some things to consider:

* If it's a bug in the decoder, you should submit a bug report on [github](https://github.com/NikitaShokhov/uniseg), preferably with a descriptive example.
* If it's a bug in an external library, a bug report should be submitted to the library owner.
* If there is man-made data in the user data, it should be filtered out.
* Depending on the context, skipping breaking characters and continuing might make sense (although for most applications it won't: you might end up with handsets being assigned to East African languages or the like if you just do that for invalid characters).
* Depending on the context, throwing an exception might make sense.

It might make sense to write a small test wrapper that decodes data and checks that it continues to work (with count checks) and that if there is an error, it throws (or continues as you wish).

In different versions of Uniseg on different languages, there are some differences on UTF decoding (except Ruby), so there is a possibility that a decoding error is a **bug**. 

For example, Ruby and Javascript versions use `String#encode` methods to encode user input (see [String#encode](http://ruby-doc.org/core-2.1.2/String.html#method-i-encode) and [String#encode](http://nodejs.org/api/buffer.html#buffer_buf_tostring_encoding_start), respectively), but python and php, only encode user data outside of uniseg.

**Python**

Decoding errors in Python version can be in **not-unicode strings** or in **unicode strings**.

HTML charsets
---

If you'll get an exception like this:

    > Traceback (most recent call last):
    >   File "test.py", line 22, in <module>
    >     u = uniseg.SegmentString(u"&#x20000;")
    >   File "/Users/nikita/src/uniseg/python/uniseg.py", line 178, in __init__
    >     self._UnicodeSegmentProps(text)
    >   File "/Users/nikita/src/uniseg/python/uniseg.py", line 143, in _UnicodeSegmentProps
    >     self._text_utf8 = text.encode('utf-8')
    >   File "/System/Library/Frameworks/Python.framework/Versions/2.7/lib/python2.7/encodings/utf_8.py", line 16, in encode
    >     return codecs.utf_8.encode(input, errors, True)
    > UnicodeEncodeError: 'ascii' codec can't encode character u'\u20000' in position 0: ordinal not in range(128)

A reason is in [issue#41](https://github.com/NikitaShokhov/uniseg/issues/41) - Python 2.x uses `ascii` codec to find encode to utf-8 or to find other fundamentally-codecs.

You should  convert user input from encoding to unicode via `imap.find()` or `imap.from_bytes()` (see [String](http://docs.python.org/2/library/string.html)) and provide your codec name:

    >>> test_text = "&#x20000;"
    >>> test_text = test_text.decode('cp1251').encode('utf8') if 'cp1251' in test_text else test_text
    >>> test_text = unicode(test_text, 'utf8') if not isinstance(test_text, unicode) else test_text
    >>> uniseg.SegmentString(test_text)
    u'「'

If you don't know a codec name of user input, you can use a parser of charsets
[178/200] Running: What are temperature vs randomness in sampling?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 What is randomness in sampling? In sampling, randomness refers to the process of selecting a sample from a population in such a way that every possible combination of sampling units has an equal probability of being selected. In other words, there is no bias or predetermined pattern in the selection of the sample.

Randomness is achieved through the use of random sampling techniques, such as simple random sampling, stratified random sampling, or cluster random sampling. These techniques use randomization methods, such as random number generation, to ensure that the selection of the sample is unbiased and representative of the population.

Temperature, on the other hand, refers to the degree of randomness in a sample. In sampling, temperature is a measure of the variability in the sampling distribution. A high-temperature sample is one in which the sampling units are highly variable, while a low-temperature sample is one in which the sampling units are more homogeneous.

Temperature is often used as a measure of the precision of a sample. A sample with a high temperature is less precise than a sample with a low temperature, because there is more variability in the sampling distribution. Therefore, a low-temperature sample is generally preferred in sampling because it is more precise and provides a more accurate estimate of the population.

To summarize, randomness in sampling refers to the process of selecting a sample in an unbiased and representative manner, while temperature refers to the degree of randomness or variability in the sampling distribution. A low-temperature sample is generally preferred because it is more precise and provides a more accurate estimate of the population.
