[192/200] Running: Summarize the main differences between supervised and unsupe...

🧠 LLM Response:


Supervised learning involves a model being trained on a labeled dataset. That is, the input data is paired with the correct output data, and the model tries to learn the relationship between the two so that it can predict the output for new, unseen input data. Supervised learning can be further divided into two categories: classification (when the output is a category) and regression (when the output is a continuous value).

Unsupervised learning, on the other hand, involves a model being trained on an unlabeled dataset. The model is not given any information about the correct output, and must instead try to find patterns or structure in the input